WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.029
Begin by framing Pipeline Foundation as a
question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:09.359
observable results.

3
00:00:09.359 --> 00:00:16.687
The OpenGL graphics pipeline processes
vertices through programmable shader stages.

4
00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:25.209
Consider this worked case: Vertex data enters
the pipeline first, where vertex shaders

5
00:00:25.209 --> 00:00:31.109
modify position, color, or normal attributes
before downstream stages.

6
00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:37.963
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

7
00:00:37.963 --> 00:00:38.453
conclusion.

8
00:00:38.453 --> 00:00:44.046
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

9
00:00:44.162 --> 00:00:51.932
Now define Primitive Assembly precisely enough
that another learner could test the

10
00:00:51.932 --> 00:00:52.580
definition.

11
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:59.518
Primitive assembly groups vertices into
points, lines, or triangles.

12
00:00:59.518 --> 00:01:06.627
Consider this worked case: Three connected
vertices form a triangle primitive, which the

13
00:01:06.627 --> 00:01:12.096
pipeline then processes as a single geometric
unit for rendering.

14
00:01:12.096 --> 00:01:18.822
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

15
00:01:18.822 --> 00:01:21.268
evidence would distinguish them.

16
00:01:21.268 --> 00:01:27.971
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

17
00:01:27.971 --> 00:01:35.014
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

18
00:01:35.014 --> 00:01:37.127
throughout the analysis.

19
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:44.298
With the foundation in place, examine how
Clipping Stage operates in a concrete

20
00:01:44.298 --> 00:01:44.840
sequence.

21
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.168
Clipping removes portions outside the view
volume.

22
00:01:49.168 --> 00:01:55.850
Consider this worked case: A triangle
extending beyond the camera's frustum is

23
00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:01.418
sliced at the clipping plane boundaries,
discarding the exterior segments.

24
00:02:01.418 --> 00:02:08.653
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

25
00:02:08.653 --> 00:02:14.340
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

26
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:21.455
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

27
00:02:21.455 --> 00:02:23.590
throughout the analysis.

28
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:30.379
Next, compare the choices involved in
Rasterization and make the decision criteria

29
00:02:30.379 --> 00:02:30.936
explicit.

30
00:02:30.936 --> 00:02:36.030
Rasterization converts primitives into
fragment candidates.

31
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:43.511
Consider this worked case: Each triangle is
subdivided into pixel-aligned fragments, with

32
00:02:43.511 --> 00:02:48.499
each fragment receiving interpolated
attributes for downstream processing.

33
00:02:48.499 --> 00:02:55.852
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

34
00:02:55.852 --> 00:02:57.077
the goalposts.

35
00:02:57.077 --> 00:03:04.077
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

36
00:03:04.077 --> 00:03:11.000
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

37
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.077
throughout the analysis.

38
00:03:13.202 --> 00:03:20.076
Apply Interpolation to a worked situation
instead of treating it as an isolated

39
00:03:20.076 --> 00:03:20.605
definition.

40
00:03:20.605 --> 00:03:26.167
Interpolation produces per-fragment values
from vertex outputs.

41
00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:34.264
Consider this worked case: Color and normal
values are linearly interpolated across a

42
00:03:34.264 --> 00:03:39.870
triangle's pixels using barycentric
coordinates to ensure smooth shading.

43
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:47.443
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

44
00:03:47.443 --> 00:03:47.948
step.

45
00:03:47.948 --> 00:03:52.901
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

46
00:03:53.018 --> 00:04:00.719
Examine the tradeoffs in Fragment Processing,
including what improves and what becomes more

47
00:04:00.719 --> 00:04:01.311
difficult.

48
00:04:01.311 --> 00:04:06.921
Fragment processing computes values for
framebuffer operations.

49
00:04:06.921 --> 00:04:13.320
Consider this worked case: Fragment shaders
evaluate lighting equations and material

50
00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:19.718
properties to generate final color and alpha
values for each pixel.

51
00:04:19.718 --> 00:04:26.749
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

52
00:04:26.749 --> 00:04:34.202
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

53
00:04:34.202 --> 00:04:41.257
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

54
00:04:41.257 --> 00:04:43.374
throughout the analysis.

55
00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:50.573
Stress-test the reasoning around Depth Testing
with an edge case and identify which

56
00:04:50.573 --> 00:04:52.752
assumption controls the outcome.

57
00:04:52.752 --> 00:04:58.424
Depth testing resolves visibility according to
stored depth values.

58
00:04:58.424 --> 00:05:04.983
Consider this worked case: Each fragment's
depth value is compared against the depth

59
00:05:04.983 --> 00:05:10.533
buffer; fragments with greater depth are
discarded to maintain correct occlusion.

60
00:05:10.533 --> 00:05:16.939
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

61
00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:23.643
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

62
00:05:23.762 --> 00:05:30.754
Use Blending to diagnose a failure by
separating symptoms, causes, and corrective

63
00:05:30.754 --> 00:05:31.337
action.

64
00:05:31.337 --> 00:05:37.087
Blending combines a fragment result with an
existing framebuffer value.

65
00:05:37.087 --> 00:05:44.536
Consider this worked case: Translucent
surfaces blend the new fragment's color with

66
00:05:44.536 --> 00:05:50.743
the underlying color using alpha values to
produce semi-transparent overlays.

67
00:05:50.743 --> 00:05:58.618
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

68
00:05:58.618 --> 00:06:00.305
smallest plausible cause.

69
00:06:00.305 --> 00:06:08.415
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

70
00:06:08.522 --> 00:06:15.706
Connect Coordinate Spaces to the earlier ideas
and explain how the pieces work as one system.

71
00:06:15.706 --> 00:06:22.409
Coordinate spaces, sampling, and numerical
precision must remain explicit.

72
00:06:22.409 --> 00:06:30.547
Consider this worked case: Explicitly defining
vertex, fragment, and texture coordinate

73
00:06:30.547 --> 00:06:37.945
spaces prevents misalignment during sampling
and ensures consistent precision across

74
00:06:37.945 --> 00:06:39.425
pipeline stages.

75
00:06:39.425 --> 00:06:48.786
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

76
00:06:48.786 --> 00:06:50.346
across representations.

77
00:06:50.346 --> 00:06:57.393
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

78
00:06:57.506 --> 00:07:04.094
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

79
00:07:04.094 --> 00:07:04.565
analysis.

80
00:07:04.565 --> 00:07:11.573
Visual correctness follows from explicit
conventions, assumptions, sampling decisions,

81
00:07:11.573 --> 00:07:13.909
and measurable error.

82
00:07:13.909 --> 00:07:21.120
Consider this worked case: Instructor-led
review checks that coordinate mappings are

83
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:28.332
explicit, sampling rates match resolution
requirements, and error bounds remain within

84
00:07:28.332 --> 00:07:29.643
acceptable thresholds.

85
00:07:29.643 --> 00:07:37.565
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

86
00:07:37.565 --> 00:07:38.784
verification step.

87
00:07:38.784 --> 00:07:45.901
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

88
00:07:45.901 --> 00:07:46.409
intuition.
